1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,853
This is STAT 331, video 5 a. The topics are maximum likelihood, fitted values, and residuals.

2
00:00:09,303 --> 00:00:15,746
This video prepares you for two question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,196 --> 00:00:28,356
Type A: derive the maximum likelihood estimators of beta zero, beta one, and sigma squared. Lecture 2 does this on slides 27 to 34.

4
00:00:28,806 --> 00:00:38,321
Type B: compute fitted values and residuals, from data or from computer output, as in Tutorial 1 and on slide 38.

5
00:00:38,771 --> 00:01:04,029
In video 4, least squares gave beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. Here are five points of our own. S x y is 6 and S x x is 10, so beta one hat is 0.6. Beta zero hat is 4 minus 0.6 times 3, which is 2.2.

6
00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:19,903
The orange segments are the vertical distances from the points to the line. Add their absolute values: 0.8 plus 0.6 plus 1 plus 0.6 plus 0.2. The sum is 3.2.

7
00:01:20,353 --> 00:01:29,292
Least squares adds the squares instead. Each orange square has the area of one squared distance. The areas add to 2.4.

8
00:01:29,742 --> 00:01:45,358
Slide 26 asks: why minimize the squares, and not another objective function? The normal model gives the answer. Maximum likelihood with this model leads to least squares. Question type A asks you to show this.

9
00:01:45,808 --> 00:01:59,461
Here is the idea of the likelihood. Take a candidate line: the green line, 2.2 plus 0.6 x. The model says: at each x i, the mean of y i is on the line.

10
00:01:59,911 --> 00:02:13,330
Around each mean, y i has a normal distribution with variance sigma squared. Each orange curve is that normal density, drawn sideways. For this picture, sigma is 0.7.

11
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:33,513
For each point, read the height of its density at the observed y i. These are the blue segments, and the green column of the table. The points are independent. So the joint density is the product of the heights. This product is the likelihood: 5.19 times 10 to the minus 3.

12
00:02:33,963 --> 00:02:37,867
Now try another line: the flat line y equals 4.

13
00:02:38,317 --> 00:02:42,477
Each point gets a new normal curve, centred on the flat line.

14
00:02:42,927 --> 00:03:08,314
Point 1 is far from the centre of its curve. Its height is only 0.010. The product is 0.13 times 10 to the minus 3. 5.19 over 0.13 is about 40. So the data are much more probable under the green line. Maximum likelihood picks the parameters that make the product as large as possible.

15
00:03:08,764 --> 00:03:25,788
Section 2. Question type A asks you to derive the maximum likelihood estimators. M L E is short for maximum likelihood estimator. Lecture 2 does it for beta zero and beta one on slides 27 to 29.

16
00:03:26,238 --> 00:03:40,382
Method card A has four steps. Step 1: write the density of each y i. The model says y i is normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared.

17
00:03:40,832 --> 00:03:50,517
Step 2: multiply the n densities. The product is the likelihood L. Then take the log. The log l turns the product into a sum.

18
00:03:50,967 --> 00:03:59,586
Step 3: take the partial derivative of l with respect to each parameter. The orange line shows a slope that is not 0.

19
00:04:00,036 --> 00:04:10,361
Step 4: set each derivative to 0, put hats on the parameters, and solve. At the top of l, the slope is 0. The solution is beta hat.

20
00:04:10,811 --> 00:04:15,142
Now we follow the card on slides 27 to 29.

21
00:04:15,592 --> 00:04:34,835
We follow card A on slide 27. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the density of one y i. It is normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared. The picture shows this density, sideways, at one x i.

22
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:50,218
Step 2: the y i are independent. So their joint density is the product of the n densities. As a function of the parameters, this product is the likelihood L. The two framed parts change in the next line.

23
00:04:50,668 --> 00:05:09,164
Each factor has the constant 1 over the square root of 2 pi sigma squared. The n equal constants give 2 pi sigma squared, to the power minus n over 2. The exponentials multiply, so their exponents add. This gives one exponential of a sum.

24
00:05:09,614 --> 00:05:32,206
Take the log. The constant gives minus n over 2 log 2 pi, and minus n over 2 log sigma squared. The log of the exponential is its exponent: minus 1 over 2 sigma squared, times the sum of squares. This is the log-likelihood, l. Only the last part contains beta zero and beta one.

25
00:05:32,656 --> 00:05:47,589
Step 2 took the log. Why is this allowed? The left plot shows the likelihood of the small data as a function of beta one. Beta zero is y bar minus beta one x bar, and sigma is 0.7.

26
00:05:48,039 --> 00:05:51,645
The right plot shows the log-likelihood of the same data.

27
00:05:52,095 --> 00:05:56,383
Both curves have the maximum at beta one equals 0.6.

28
00:05:56,833 --> 00:06:13,195
The log function is increasing. A larger L always gives a larger l. So the maximum of l is at the same point as the maximum of L. The log also turns the product into a sum. A sum is easier to differentiate.

29
00:06:13,645 --> 00:06:34,360
Step 3 of card A, slide 28: take the partial derivatives. Pause the video and try it. Write u i for y i minus beta zero minus beta one x i. Only the last part of l contains beta zero and beta one. The framed u i squared changes next.

30
00:06:34,810 --> 00:06:54,906
The derivative with respect to beta zero. Use the chain rule on u i squared. The outer derivative is 2 u i. The inner derivative of u i with respect to beta zero is minus 1, in orange. On the right, this derivative is the slope of l along beta zero.

31
00:06:55,356 --> 00:07:08,049
Simplify. Minus one half, times 2, times minus 1, is plus 1. So the derivative is 1 over sigma squared, times the sum of the u i. Call this equation star.

32
00:07:08,499 --> 00:07:20,361
The derivative with respect to beta one. The chain rule again. Now the inner derivative is minus x i. On the right, this is the slope of l along beta one.

33
00:07:20,811 --> 00:07:34,805
Minus one half, times 2, times minus x i, is plus x i. So the derivative is 1 over sigma squared, times the sum of u i times x i. Call this equation double star.

34
00:07:35,255 --> 00:07:55,266
The derivative with respect to sigma squared. Treat sigma squared as one variable, v. Minus n over 2 log v gives minus n over 2 v. Minus the sum over 2 v gives plus the sum over 2 v squared. On the right, this is the slope of l along sigma squared.

35
00:07:55,716 --> 00:08:11,439
Step 4: set the derivatives to 0. In star, multiply both sides by sigma squared, and put hats on the betas. The sum of y i minus beta zero hat minus beta one hat x i is 0.

36
00:08:11,889 --> 00:08:17,371
Do the same with double star. The sum of the same terms, times x i, is 0.

37
00:08:17,821 --> 00:08:39,027
Why is this least squares? For a fixed sigma squared, l is a constant minus S over 2 sigma squared. S is the sum of squares of video 4. On the right, a smaller S gives a larger l. Slope 0 gives S equal to 6. Slope 0.3 gives 3.3. Slope 1 gives 4.

38
00:08:39,477 --> 00:09:02,943
So the maximum of l is at the minimum of S. Lines 1 and 2 are the least squares equations of video 4. Their solution is beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. For the small data, the minimum, S equals 2.4, is at slope 0.6.

39
00:09:03,393 --> 00:09:14,849
So the least squares estimators and the maximum likelihood estimators are equal. From now on, we call them beta hat. Question type B uses them in step 1.

40
00:09:15,299 --> 00:09:22,937
Section 3 defines two quantities. Question type B asks you to compute them. Here are the small data again.

41
00:09:23,387 --> 00:09:35,931
The fitted value, y i hat, is the point on the fitted line at x i: beta zero hat plus beta one hat x i. The hollow green circles are the fitted values.

42
00:09:36,381 --> 00:09:51,207
The residual, e i, is the observed value minus the fitted value: y i minus y i hat. It is the vertical distance from the point to the fitted line. A point above the line has a positive residual.

43
00:09:51,657 --> 00:09:57,439
The table gives all the values for the fitted line 2.2 plus 0.6 x.

44
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:12,459
For example, point 1 has x equal to 1. y 1 hat is 2.2 plus 0.6, which is 2.8. y 1 is 2. So e 1 is 2 minus 2.8, which is minus 0.8.

45
00:10:12,909 --> 00:10:25,944
Slide 31 says: the residuals are different from the errors. These eight points come from our own simulation. We chose the true line: beta zero is 1 and beta one is 0.8.

46
00:10:26,394 --> 00:10:39,685
The errors, epsilon i, are the vertical distances from the points to the true line. They are the gray dashed segments. In real data, the true line is unknown, so the errors are not observed.

47
00:10:40,135 --> 00:10:49,201
The residuals, e i, are the distances to the fitted line. They are the orange segments. We can always calculate them from the data.

48
00:10:49,651 --> 00:10:56,862
For point 3, the error and the residual are different numbers, because the fitted line is not the true line.

49
00:10:57,312 --> 00:11:08,171
The residuals add to 0, as the next slide shows. Here the errors add to minus 4.54. We use the residuals to learn about the errors.

50
00:11:08,621 --> 00:11:21,570
Equations star and double star also give two facts about the residuals. Line 1 is star, set to 0 at the estimates. The framed bracket is y i minus y i hat.

51
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:40,281
So each term is the residual e i, and the residuals add to 0. The bars show the five residuals of the small data: minus 0.8, 0.6, 1, minus 0.6, and minus 0.2. Their sum is 0.

52
00:11:40,731 --> 00:11:49,947
Line 3 is double star, set to 0. Again the framed bracket is e i. The purple bars show x i times e i.

53
00:11:50,397 --> 00:12:00,275
So the sum of x i times e i is 0: minus 0.8 plus 1.2 plus 3, minus 2.4, minus 1.

54
00:12:00,725 --> 00:12:10,773
These are two linear constraints on the residuals. Card B uses them as a check. Video 5 b uses them to explain the n minus 2.

55
00:12:11,223 --> 00:12:21,953
Question type B asks you to compute fitted values and residuals. Tutorial 1 and slide 38 ask this. Method card B has four steps.

56
00:12:22,403 --> 00:12:31,214
Step 1: write the fitted line, beta zero hat plus beta one hat x. Compute the estimates, or read them from the output.

57
00:12:31,664 --> 00:12:38,683
Step 2: for each x i, compute the fitted value y i hat. The hollow circles are on the line.

58
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:46,685
Step 3: the residual is y i minus y i hat. A point above the line has a positive residual.

59
00:12:47,135 --> 00:12:54,452
Step 4: check. The residuals add to 0, and x i times e i add to 0, up to rounding.

60
00:12:54,902 --> 00:13:11,073
Now a tutorial question of type B. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 1 c, asks for the standard error of beta one hat. Its first step is the fitted values and the residuals. We compute them now.

61
00:13:11,523 --> 00:13:38,659
Step 1 of card B: the fitted line from part a. Use the unrounded estimates. Beta one hat is minus 531 over 1294, which is minus 0.410355. Beta zero hat is 27 plus 0.410355 times 101, which is 68.4459.

62
00:13:39,109 --> 00:13:56,431
Step 2: the fitted value for month 1. x 1 is 110. 68.4459 minus 0.410355 times 110 is 23.307.

63
00:13:56,881 --> 00:14:02,236
The same for every month. The hollow green circles are the seven fitted values.

64
00:14:02,686 --> 00:14:17,555
Step 3: the residual for month 1. y 1 is 26. 26 minus 23.307 is 2.693. The point is above the line, so the residual is positive.

65
00:14:18,005 --> 00:14:29,611
The same for every month. The orange segments are the seven residuals. The month with 88 hours has the largest residual: 7.665.

66
00:14:30,061 --> 00:14:37,826
Step 4: check. The residuals add to minus 0.001. This is 0 up to rounding.

67
00:14:38,276 --> 00:14:50,649
The products x i times e i also add to 0. So the fitted values and residuals are correct. Video 5 b uses these residuals to compute sigma hat.

68
00:14:51,099 --> 00:15:06,353
Slide 38 gives R output for the Gladstone data: 236 people, head size and brain weight. Pause the video and try it. Check the first fitted value, and find the residuals of the first six people.

69
00:15:06,803 --> 00:15:26,792
Step 1 of card B: read the fitted line from the output. y hat equals 335.4323 plus 0.2608207 x. The table shows the first six fitted values from R. The hollow circles are on the line.

70
00:15:27,242 --> 00:15:49,685
Step 2: check y 1 hat. x 1 is 4512. 335.4323 plus 0.2608207 times 4512 is 1512.255. This agrees with the output.

71
00:15:50,135 --> 00:16:06,519
Step 3: the residual of person 1. y 1 is 1530. 1530 minus 1512.255 is 17.745 grams.

72
00:16:06,969 --> 00:16:22,115
The same for the other people. Person 5 has the largest residual of the six: 165.120 grams. Person 3 is 111.789 grams below the line.

73
00:16:22,565 --> 00:16:28,667
Step 4: check. The 236 residuals of the data file add to 0.

74
00:16:29,117 --> 00:16:47,293
Section 4 uses card A for the third parameter, sigma squared. This is slide 34. Pause the video and try it. Step 3 gave the derivative of l with respect to sigma squared. On the right is l as a function of sigma squared, for the small data.

75
00:16:47,743 --> 00:16:56,575
Step 4: set the derivative to 0. Put in the estimates beta zero hat and beta one hat, and put a hat on sigma squared.

76
00:16:57,025 --> 00:17:08,523
Multiply both sides by 2 times sigma squared hat, squared. The first term becomes minus n sigma squared hat. The second term becomes the sum of squares.

77
00:17:08,973 --> 00:17:19,085
Solve for sigma squared hat. The maximum likelihood estimator is the sum of squares divided by n. The framed bracket changes next.

78
00:17:19,535 --> 00:17:27,429
Each bracket is a residual, e i. So sigma squared hat M L is the sum of e i squared, over n.

79
00:17:27,879 --> 00:17:43,452
For the small data, the squared residuals add to 2.4, and n is 5. So sigma squared hat M L is 2.4 over 5, which is 0.48. The curve has its maximum at 0.48.

80
00:17:43,902 --> 00:17:53,801
This is the maximum likelihood estimator of sigma squared. Video 5 b shows that we usually divide by n minus 2 instead.

81
00:17:54,251 --> 00:18:14,453
To recap. Card A derives the maximum likelihood estimators: the density, the likelihood and its log, the partial derivatives, and the solution. For beta zero and beta one, the result is the least squares estimates. For sigma squared, it is the sum of squared residuals over n.

82
00:18:14,903 --> 00:18:28,365
Card B computes fitted values and residuals: the fitted line, the fitted values, the residuals, and the check with the two sums. Residuals come from the fitted line. Errors come from the true line.

83
00:18:28,815 --> 00:18:34,959
Video 5 b shows the estimator that divides by n minus 2, and why we use it.
